AU  - S Saeedan Abdulaziz
AU  - Ansari Mohd Nazam
JO  - ACS pharmacology & translational science
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1021/acsptsci.6c00440
AB  - Diabetes-associated cognitive dysfunction (DACD), which includes memory loss, executive dysfunction, attention, processing speed, and an increased risk of dementia, is a growing problem associated with diabetes mellitus (DM), especially type 2 diabetes mellitus. Due to a significant increase in the global burden of diabetes and population ageing, DACD is becoming an important public health issue. This review provides a comprehensive overview of the pathophysiology, molecular mechanisms, biomarkers, experimental models, and emerging therapeutic strategies associated with DACD. At present, it is believed that the multiple mechanisms associated with chronic hyperglycemia, insulin resistance, oxidative stress, mitochondrial dysfunction, neuroinflammation, blood-brain barrier disruption, and microvascular injury all contribute to the pathogenesis of DACD. These interrelated mechanisms are responsible for cognitive decline, synaptic dysfunction, and neuronal damage. Experimental research has shown that specific molecular pathways contribute to the pathogenesis of DACD, such as AGE-RAGE signaling, nuclear factor-κB activation, PI3K/Akt dysregulation, and inflammatory cascades. Emerging biomarkers including neurofilament light (NfL) chain, phosphorylated tau (p-tau), neuroimaging markers, exosomes, metabolomic profiles, and transcriptomic signatures may improve early diagnosis and risk stratification. Several therapeutic approaches, including GLP-1 receptor agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors, DPP-4 inhibitors, intranasal insulin, nanoparticle-based delivery systems, stem cell therapies, and bioactive compounds, have demonstrated promising neuroprotective effects in preclinical studies, although clinical translation remains limited. Finally, future studies should be directed toward the use of biomarker-driven diagnosis, clinically relevant disease models, and large-scale clinical studies in order to develop effective therapeutic interventions. Understanding more of the mechanisms that underlie DACD could help inform the creation of precision medicine strategies to prevent or delay diabetes-related cognitive decline.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42871641/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 42860584
TI  - Diabetes-Associated Cognitive Impairment: Pathophysiology and Emerging Therapies.
AU  - Corbali Osman
AU  - Menon Priyanka
AU  - Corbali Halil I
AU  - Jagannathan Ram
AU  - Levey Allan I
JO  - Annals of clinical and translational neurology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1002/acn3.70487
AB  - Diabetes-associated cognitive impairment (DACI) is a common yet underrecognized condition, manifesting along a spectrum from mild cognitive impairment to dementia. DACI encompasses patients who develop vascular dementia (VaD) or Alzheimer's disease (AD) with diabetes as a risk factor; however, in certain patients, particularly those lacking clear VaD or AD features, diabetes may act as a primary driver of neurodegeneration. Mechanistically, DACI can be conceptualized as a neurovascular-metabolic disorder characterized by disruption of the neurovascular unit, impaired cerebral glucose metabolism, brain insulin resistance, and chronic neuroinflammation, contributing to small vessel disease, regional brain atrophy, and accelerated brain aging. In this narrative review, we synthesize current evidence on the clinical and imaging features of DACI, outline key mechanistic pathways linking diabetes to neurodegeneration, and discuss emerging therapeutic strategies, including GLP-1 receptor agonists and SGLT2 inhibitors, with potential neuroprotective effects. Greater recognition of DACI as a neurovascular-metabolic entity may facilitate earlier identification, inform biomarker development, and guide future trials aimed at preserving cognitive function in individuals with diabetes.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42860584/
